== SOURCE
u:l_ife := { ('+ r_/_12 -1 0 1 o_-_12 _r) { (_l = 3) + _r * _l = 4 } _r }
== TITLE
Conway's Life in one line, read right to left
== NOTE u:l_ife
Name: life
u: puts the name in the program's own namespace (a raised u); the underlined l makes it a function.
== NOTE :=
Binding
Gives the name life to the function on its right.
== NOTE {
A function { ... }
Its right argument ⍵ (typed _r) is the board: a matrix of 0s (dead) and 1s (alive).
== NOTE '+
Plus, quoted
The quote passes + as a value: the operand of the reduce on its right.
== NOTE r_/_12
Reduce over axes 1 and 2
Sums the 3 by 3 shifted boards cell by cell: each cell becomes S, itself plus its 8 neighbors.
== NOTE -1 0 1
The offsets
One strand of three numbers: shift back one, not at all, forward one.
== NOTE o_-_12
Rotate on axes 1 and 2
Rotates the board by every offset along both axes (every combination): 3 by 3 boards, each shifted one of 9 ways.
== NOTE _r
⍵, the board
Right to left, the first value used: the board that is shifted.
== NOTE { #2
A second function
Called as S {...} board: dyadic, so inside it ⍺ (typed _l) is S and ⍵ is the board.
== NOTE _l = 3
⍺ = 3
1 where S is 3: a dead cell with 3 live neighbors, or a live one with 2. Alive next, either way.
== NOTE + #2
Either
The two cases never hold at once (S is not 3 and 4), so adding them is an or: 1 alive, 0 dead.
== NOTE _r * _l = 4
⍵ × ⍺ = 4
Right to left: ⍺ = 4 first, then times the board ⍵: a live cell with 3 live neighbors stays alive.
== NOTE _r #3
⍵, the board again
The second function's right argument: the same board, so each cell counts in its own S.
